+# Privacy architecture and resource audit
+
+The site is informational. It has no accounts, checkout, analytics, cookies, fingerprinting, personal data collection, or server data store. The application does not log request paths, addresses, identifiers, or bodies.
+
+## Data inventory
+
+One optional localStorage key, `daily-grind-privacy-v1`, holds `{version, remember, expiresAt}` after an explicit user action. No personal data or tokens are stored. Retention is 30 days; expired records are removed on next access. Disabling remembering, resetting, or clearing data deletes the record. Export is produced locally as JSON. Browser storage failures default to no persistence.
+
+## Third-party technical review
+
+- Unsplash photograph (`photo-1447933601403-0c6688de566e`): downloaded to `assets/coffee.jpg`. No client-side connection to Unsplash. Source: https://unsplash.com. Keep license records if replacing the asset.
+- Fonts: operating-system fonts; no font service.
+- JavaScript/CSS: local, no third-party code or runtime npm dependencies.
+- Embeds, SDKs, ads, analytics: none.
+- Hosting: not selected in this repository. Review the selected provider's processing terms, subprocessors, access controls, request logging, retention, deletion, and TLS configuration before release.
+
+This is an internal source/resource audit, not an independent audit or certification. Run `npm test` after every change, and review third-party integrations before adding them. Review provider terms and data flows at least quarterly. The tests enforce local resources and disallow common tracking and unsafe DOM APIs; they do not certify every possible tracking technique.
+
+## Security and deployment
+
+Use `npm start` (or `pnpm start`) to serve the application through `server.mjs`, not a generic static server that bypasses the policies. Every response gets CSP, Permissions Policy, no-referrer, nosniff, frame protections, COOP, CORP, no-store, and Fetch Metadata isolation. HTML has a fresh cryptographic script nonce. Only public assets are served, with no filesystem traversal. Unsupported methods and cross-site non-navigation resource requests are rejected. No security reporting endpoint is installed, avoiding collection of potentially sensitive report URLs or samples.
+
+Terminate TLS at a trusted reverse proxy/host; redirect HTTP to HTTPS there. Set `HTTPS_ONLY=1` only once the public site is exclusively HTTPS; this enables a short initial HSTS duration (300 seconds). Increase the duration after validating deployment, without adding includeSubDomains or preload until all relevant domains are ready. Configure host access-log minimization and retention separately. Do not expose the HTTP origin directly in production.
+
+There are no authentication sessions to clear or protect with cookies. Future accounts or checkout require a separate data inventory, access controls, encryption in transit/at rest, secret management, retention rules, secure HttpOnly session cookies, logout cleanup, and renewed vendor review before activation.
